# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the WallpaperX daemon.
#
# Everything here is a *third-party* library TSan cannot see into, not a WallpaperX
# race. Nothing in backend/ or renderer/ belongs in this file -- if a report names our
# own code, it is a finding, not noise. That rule survives intact; read on before adding
# an exception to it.
#
# THE LOADER ARTIFACT IS HANDLED BY A FLAG, NOT BY AN ENTRY HERE. `vkDestroyDevice` and
# `vkDestroyInstance` unlock a loader-internal mutex TSan never saw locked, and because
# the loader is uninstrumented TSan attributes the unlock to the nearest instrumented
# frame -- `Device::destroyResources()`, ours. `called_from_lib:` cannot catch it for
# exactly that reason. llvmpipe's LLVM thread pool produces the same shape through
# `std::condition_variable::wait`, attributed to libstdc++. Both are mutex-*misuse*
# reports, not races, so `report_mutex_bugs=0` (set on the TSan tests by
# backend/CMakeLists.txt) removes the whole class without naming a single one of our
# functions. Measured 2026-08-04: with it, data races between instrumented threads, the
# condition_variable use-after-destroy, and a race placed inside `destroyResources`
# itself are all still reported.
#
# What that costs, stated plainly: TSan no longer reports double-lock, unlock-of-unlocked
# or destroy-while-locked anywhere. Data race detection -- the reason this suite runs TSan
# at all -- is untouched.
#
# WHAT NOT TO REACH FOR: `ignore_noninstrumented_modules=1` looks like the purpose-built
# answer here, and it does silence the loader artifact. Do not use it. Measured
# 2026-08-04: it also silences a use-after-destroy on a `std::condition_variable`
# notified by a live thread -- 2 reports without it, 0 with -- because the notify path
# runs through libstdc++/libc. That is precisely the shape of the `NameWatcher` bug this
# suite found, so the flag blinds the gate to the bug class it exists to catch. It leaves
# plain races between instrumented threads visible, which is what makes it so convincing
# and so dangerous.

# The Khronos validation layer. Uninstrumented, and it keeps its own concurrent state
# maps behind rwlocks TSan cannot pair up. This is the loudest source by far, and it is
# present only because the tests ask for validation -- the shipped daemon does not load
# it at all.
called_from_lib:libVkLayer_khronos_validation.so

# Mesa / Lavapipe. Uninstrumented, and llvmpipe's rasteriser uses its own worker pool
# with atomics and futexes TSan reads as unsynchronised.
called_from_lib:libvulkan_lvp.so
called_from_lib:libvulkan.so
called_from_lib:libLLVM

# The Vulkan loader spawns nothing itself but dlopen's ICDs whose init TSan attributes
# to whichever thread got there first.
called_from_lib:libvulkan_radeon.so
called_from_lib:libvulkan_intel.so

# sd-bus runs the connection's event loop; its internal reference counting is
# uninstrumented and shows up on every signal emission from the render thread.
called_from_lib:libsystemd.so
